[{:db/id "q-353452/field-1/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" :answer/id #uuid "d3631dab-9cc0-588c-b563-72fe8cee6bab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353452/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa6a76ed-6418-5cda-8e27-b1d632041b70" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353452/field-1/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279" :answer-field/choices ["q-353452/field-1/4e0d8402ce61a9969d16a551582a4c98e6792e5edb3c1272d991dba7d5c92279"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353452" :question/id #uuid "952f2d49-202a-52d4-9191-44f2632fec2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-353452" :question/problem "A reservoir releases $14$ cubic meters of water every hour. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V(t)$ remaining in the reservoir? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in hours.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If the volume of water remaining is $V,$ then the rate of change of the volume is $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in hours.\n\nSince $14$ cubic meters of water are released each hour, the rate of change of the volume is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-14.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is negative because the volume of water is *decreasing*." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353452/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353452"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-38308/selection/43c7bb963920aa0c61fc60cd916a3aad7e57442f004f6471721938c01799fbda" :answer/id #uuid "601964eb-2d0f-57a4-8aa5-bf28e86a3e72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=-1" :d